# Handling POST Data

`req.body` is not available by default. You need to parse the incoming stream.

**Simple way:** Use a helper or the built‑in `req` events.

Next.js doesn't include a body parser out of the box, but you can write a small middleware or use the following pattern:

```js
const body = await new Promise((resolve) => {
  let data = '';
  req.on('data', chunk => data += chunk);
  req.on('end', () => resolve(JSON.parse(data)));
});
```

*For convenience, many use `micro` or other tiny libraries.*

slides.codeSnippet:
// pages/api/feedback.js
const feedbacks = [];

export default async function handler(req, res) {
  if (req.method === 'POST') {
    // Parse JSON body
    let body = '';
    req.on('data', chunk => body += chunk);
    req.on('end', () => {
      const { message } = JSON.parse(body);
      feedbacks.push({ id: Date.now(), message });
      res.status(201).json({ success: true });
    });
  } else {
    res.status(200).json(feedbacks);
  }
}
